        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Abstract: Propiolaldehyde was prepared by the oxidation of propargyl alcohol using molecular oxygen as the oxidant under atmospheric pressure and room temperature in a decent yield. Compared to the previous method with "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "{ \\mathrm { C r O } } _ { 3 } ,"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", such a protocol avoids the use of a stoichiometric amount of oxidant, and the workup procedure is much more eco-friendly and convenient affording a higher yield of the product. "
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                77,
                268,
                917,
                307
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Key words: propargyl alcohol, aerobic oxidation, iron nitrate, TEMPO, sodium chloride "
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                78,
                309,
                620,
                324
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "image",
            "content": {
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/657c39327e02216d00c9c62874a0f60ee6f9e45c17aa657a73a1e81241e7b490.jpg"
                },
                "content": "Organic reaction scheme showing oxidation of a hydroxy alcohol to an enone using Fe(NO3)3·9H2O and TEMPO under NaCl conditions",
                "image_caption": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Scheme 1 Iron-catalyzed aerobic oxidation of propargyl alcohol "
                    }
                ],
                "image_footnote": []
            },
            "sub_type": "chemical",
            "bbox": [
                255,
                372,
                742,
                441
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Propiolaldehyde is an important and useful chemical intermediate for organic synthesis, especially for the Michael addition,1 nucleophilic addition,2 Diels–Alder reaction,3 [3+2] cyclization,4 Sonogashira coupling,5 as well as other reactions6 (Scheme 2). "
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                77,
                485,
                489,
                555
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "image",
            "content": {
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/0abe43631c8cfc421a05b2b8e9f6ea805bc98b85ffb0dc9c9bff8a59d6475286.jpg"
                },
                "content": "Reaction mechanism diagram showing sonogashira coupling and nucleophilic addition pathways for a heterocyclic compound",
                "image_caption": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Scheme 2 Reactions of propiolaldehyde "
                    }
                ],
                "image_footnote": []
            },
            "sub_type": "chemical",
            "bbox": [
                80,
                571,
                484,
                771
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Traditionally, propiolaldehyde is prepared by the oxidation of propargyl alcohol using at least a stoichiometric amount of oxidants, such as "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathrm { { \\bar { C } r O } } _ { 3 } , ^ { 7 }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "MnO28 as well as "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathrm { D M S O . } ^ { 9 }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "In 1956, Sauer reported a practical method for the oxidation of propargyl alcohol using "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathrm { C r O } _ { 3 }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "as oxidant affording propiolaldehyde in 35–41% yield.7a However, this oxidation system would produce almost a quantitative amount of oxidant-derived chromium waste, which is difficult to remove and the workup procedure is not so convenient for isolation. Therefore, novel protocols for this oxidation are highly required. "
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                504,
                485,
                917,
                641
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "As a cheap and mild oxidant, molecular oxygen has drawn more and more attention.10 Recently, our group has developed an eco-friendly, mild, and practical aerobic oxidation protocol for alcohols bearing various types of functional groups including allenic, benzylic, allylic, as well as propargylic alcohols using "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "\\mathrm { F e } ( \\mathrm { N O } _ { 3 } ) _ { 3 } { \\cdot } 9 \\mathrm { H } _ { 2 } \\mathrm { O } _ { \\cdot }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": ", TEMPO and NaCl as catalysts under atmospheric pressure and room temperature in 1,2-dichloroethane11 or toluene.12 Compared to Sauer’s method, this aerobic oxidation protocol is much more eco-friendly and convenient. Thus, we have now applied this protocol to the synthesis of propiolaldehyde. "
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                504,
                643,
                917,
                813
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Based on our previous observation, 1,2-dichloroethane is the best solvent for this reaction. However, the boiling point of propiolaldehyde is close to DCE, and it would be difficult to isolate. Therefore, an alternative solvent for this reaction was chosen. "
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "equation_inline",
                        "content": "p { \\mathrm { - } } { \\mathrm { X y } }"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "lene is a commonly used organic solvent with a high boiling point. Fortunately, when p-xylene was used as reaction solvent instead of DCE, 54% isolated yield of propiolaldehyde was obtained after only simple workup procedure and distillation (Scheme 1). "
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                504,
                815,
                917,
                887
            ]
        },
